---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
/**
 * SPEC §14.10 — "state this in a short privacy note linked in the footer".
 *
 * ## This page is a promise, so it changed when the behaviour did
 *
 * It used to import `RETENTION_MONTHS` from the pruning job, so the stated
 * window could not drift from the window actually enforced. There is no
 * database and no pruning job now: a message is emailed and not stored, which
 * makes the retention section a statement about an inbox rather than about a
 * table. Nothing here may describe the old architecture — a privacy note that
 * over-states what is collected is as wrong as one that under-states it.
 *
 * ## Voice
 *
 * BRAND §8: plain, specific, slightly dry. No "we value your privacy", no
 * cookie-banner boilerplate for cookies the site does not set. It says what is
 * collected, what is done to it, how long it stays, and how to have it removed.
 * Anything longer would be padding, and padding is what makes these unread.
 */
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy — mh.neri.ph"
  description="What the contact form collects, how long it is kept, and how to have it deleted."
>
  <article class="flex max-w-[68ch] flex-col gap-lg py-xl sm:py-2xl">
    <h1 class="font-display text-headline-lg">Privacy</h1>

    <p>
      This site has no analytics, no advertising, and no third-party embeds. It
      sets one cookie, and only after you sign in to the admin area — there is
      no cookie banner because there is nothing to consent to.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-headline-sm font-display">
      What the contact form collects
    </h2>
    <p>
      Your name, your email address, and your message. Nothing else is asked
      for, and nothing is inferred.
    </p>
    <p>
      One thing is derived alongside it: a salted SHA-256 hash of your IP
      address — <strong>never the address itself</strong> — which is what the rate
      limit counts against. It is held in memory for the length of the rate-limit
      window and never written to disk. The hash cannot be reversed to an address,
      and no readable form of it exists at any point.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-headline-sm font-display">Where it goes</h2>
    <p>
      Your message is emailed to one inbox — mine. This site has no database, so
      no copy is kept here: once the email is sent, nothing about your
      submission remains on the server. It is not shared, sold, or passed to
      anyone else. Server logs record that a submission happened; they do not
      record the message, your name, your email address, or your IP.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-headline-sm font-display">How long it is kept</h2>
    <p>
      For as long as the email sits in my inbox, which is a mailbox I read
      rather than a system with a retention policy. There is no automated
      deletion, and claiming otherwise would be inventing a schedule nothing
      enforces.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-headline-sm font-display">Having it deleted</h2>
    <p>
      Ask, and it goes. Send the request through the <a
        class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-deep-teal focus-visible:text-deep-teal"
        href="/#contact">contact form</a
      > — say you want your earlier message removed and roughly when you sent it.
      No account, no form to fill in, no waiting period.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
